Post-order visits the left subtree, the right subtree, then the root. In DEBFCA the last node, $A$, is the root, and the node just before it, $C$, is the root of $A$'s only subtree; $B$ and $F$ come before $C$, and $D$, $E$ before $B$.

The post-order sequence alone does **not** determine the tree (an in-order sequence would be needed). Enumerating by script every binary tree whose post-order is DEBFCA gives 132 trees with 21 different pre-orders. Among the printed options:

- ACBDFE is impossible (it cannot be the pre-order of any such tree);
- ACBFDG contains a node $G$ that is not in the tree;
- ACBDEF is the pre-order of the natural tree $A \to C \to (B(D, E),\ F)$, where $B$ has the leaves $D$ and $E$ and $C$ has the leaf $F$ (post-order $D\,E\,B\,F\,C\,A$);
- ACBEDF is also compatible with one less natural tree (the node $E$ having $D$ as its only child).

The answer expected from the examiner is the pre-order of the natural tree, ACBDEF.

**Intended answer: C (ACBDEF).**

The printed question is not reliable enough to be marked automatically so it is left open for self-assessment instead of being auto-marked.
